<p>Little River-Academy is a small city southeast of Temple, close enough that many residents commute into town every day. It combines the former communities of Little River and Academy, and it is surrounded by farmland. From our base in Temple, it is a quick drive, so scheduling an inspection there is no harder than anywhere in town.</p>

<h2>Homes and Roofs in Little River-Academy</h2>
<p>Most homes here are single-family houses, many on larger lots than you would find in Temple. You will see older ranch homes, newer houses on the edges of town, and manufactured homes on rural parcels. Detached garages, carports and metal outbuildings are common.</p>
<p>That mix leads to a few recurring roofing needs:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Aging shingle roofs.</strong> Older homes may be on a roof that has seen many summers. Brittle shingles, granule loss and curling edges are signs a replacement is getting close.</li>
<li><strong>Low-slope sections and additions.</strong> Porch roofs, carports and room additions are sometimes built at a lower pitch than the main roof. Where the two meet is a frequent leak point.</li>
<li><strong>Metal outbuildings.</strong> Barns and sheds with exposed-fastener metal panels need fastener checks and occasional panel replacement after storms.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Weather Exposure Southeast of Temple</h2>
<p>The open country around Little River-Academy leaves roofs exposed to the full force of passing storms. Spring brings the highest risk of hail and straight-line winds, with another round of severe weather possible in the fall. Heavy rain can arrive quickly, and roofs with clogged or undersized gutters can push water behind fascia boards and into walls. Long stretches of summer heat dry out sealants and shingles, which is why many leaks show up during the first big storm after a hot, dry spell.</p>

<h2>Simple Checks Between Storms</h2>
<p>You don't need a ladder to keep an eye on your roof. A few times a year, and after any strong storm, walk around the house and look for shingles that are missing, curled or a different color from the rest. Check that gutters are draining and that downspouts aren't dumping water against the foundation. Inside, look at ceilings under the attic and around vent pipes for new stains. If you see something, take a photo with the date. Those notes help us find the source faster.</p>

